 $\\boldsymbol{v}_1$ as it is","Swap the roles of $U$ and $V$ in the product","Negate both $\\boldsymbol{u}_1$ and $\\boldsymbol{v}_1$","List $\\sigma_2$ before $\\sigma_1$, keeping $U$ and $V$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5f8ab5ea21f73b8a195a9e7f","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:27:10.718416+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_decomposition_11a598e3_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A real $2\\times3$ matrix $A$ has singular values $5$ and $2$. Which matrix is $\\Sigma$ in a full SVD $A=U\\Sigma V^{\\top}$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}5&0\\\\0&2\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}25&0&0\\\\0&4&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2&0&0\\\\0&5&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}5&0&0\\\\0&2&0\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b1d1907380111cde3fb3454f","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:27:10.718416+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_decomposition_11a598e3_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-2)$, symmetric with orthogonal diagonalization $A=I\\,\\operatorname{diag}(1,-2)\\,I^{\\top}$. Is this factorization an SVD of $A$?","options":["Yes: every orthogonal diagonalization is also an SVD of the matrix","No, since the middle factor has the negative entry $-2$","Yes, once the diagonal is reordered to $\\operatorname{diag}(-2,1)$","No: a symmetric matrix with a negative eigenvalue has no SVD"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ac0d943789775277c6f45dae","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:27:10.718416+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_decomposition_11a598e3_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A=U\\Sigma V^{\\top}$. The columns $\\boldsymbol{v}_i$ of $V$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of which matrix?","options":["$AA^{\\top}$, with eigenvalues $\\sigma_i^2$ (and $0$)","$A$ itself, with eigenvalues $\\sigma_i$","$A^{\\top}A$, with eigenvalues $\\sigma_i^2$ (and $0$)","$A+A^{\\top}$, with eigenvalues $2\\sigma_i$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"252c2a2643bbad9ff1f34c57","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:27:10.718416+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_decomposition_11a598e3_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=U\\Sigma V^{\\top}$ be a singular value decomposition of a real invertible $2\\times2$ matrix, with $\\Sigma=\\operatorname{diag}(4,2)$, columns $\\boldsymbol{u}_1,\\boldsymbol{u}_2$ of $U$ and $\\boldsymbol{v}_1,\\boldsymbol{v}_2$ of $V$. What is $A^{-1}\\boldsymbol{u}_1$?","options":["$4\\boldsymbol{v}_1$","$\\tfrac{1}{4}\\boldsymbol{v}_1$","$\\tfrac{1}{4}\\boldsymbol{u}_1$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}\\boldsymbol{v}_2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0348fe877fc26d43af5c0b59","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:27:10.718416+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_decomposition_11a598e3_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $Q$ be a $2\\times2$ orthogonal matrix and $A=2Q$. Which statement about the singular value decompositions $A=U\\Sigma V^{\\top}$ of $A$ is correct?","options":["The SVD is unique, as the singular values are fixed","Every orthogonal $V$ works, with $U=QV$ and $\\Sigma=2I$","Only $V=I$ works, with $U=Q$ and $\\Sigma=2I$","$V$ must consist of eigenvectors of $Q$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a43b3f767a01d9dd08ec8678","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:27:10.718416+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_decomposition_11a598e3_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In the constructive proof of the SVD for a real $m\\times n$ matrix of rank $r<m$, the vectors $\\boldsymbol{u}_i=A\\boldsymbol{v}_i/\\sigma_i$ are used only for $i\\le r$. How are the remaining columns of $U$ obtained?","options":["By the same formula, since $A\\boldsymbol{v}_i/\\sigma_i$ works for all $i$","As further eigenvectors of $A^{\\top}A$, which lie in $\\mathbb{R}^m$","By completing $\\boldsymbol{u}_1,\\ldots,\\boldsymbol{u}_r$ to an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^m$","They are left zero, since $\\sigma_i=0$ for $i>r$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1e2c75777af71a1d0736e9e0","time_limit":45,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:27:10.718416+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_decomposition_11a598e3_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"The matrix $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-2\\\\3&0\\end{pmatrix}$ maps the unit circle $\\{\\boldsymbol{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\colon\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|=1\\}$ onto an ellipse. Using its singular value decomposition, what are the semi-axes of this ellipse?","options":["Length $3$ along the $x$-axis and length $2$ along the $y$-axis","Length $3$ along the $y$-axis and length $2$ along the $x$-axis","Length $9$ along the $y$-axis and length $4$ along the $x$-axis","A circle of radius $\\sqrt{6}$: all semi-axes have length $\\sqrt{6}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9536d85e0d8084af1e74c5d7","time_limit":50,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:27:10.718416+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_decomposition_11a598e3_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"$A=\\begin{pmatrix}3&0\\\\4&5\\end{pmatrix}$ has singular values $\\sigma_1=3\\sqrt{5}$, $\\sigma_2=\\sqrt{5}$ with $\\boldsymbol{v}_1=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(1,1)^{\\top}$, $\\boldsymbol{u}_1=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}(1,3)^{\\top}$ and $\\boldsymbol{v}_2=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(1,-1)^{\\top}$. Which $\\boldsymbol{u}_2$ completes the singular value decomposition?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{u}_2=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}(3,-1)^{\\top}$","$\\boldsymbol{u}_2=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}(-3,1)^{\\top}$","$\\boldsymbol{u}_2=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(3,-1)^{\\top}$","$\\boldsymbol{u}_2=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(1,-1)^{\\top}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6b8a7b2f1bf179442d83b7cf","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-26T13:27:10.718416+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}